# 🌐 IPv4 Address Types – Quick Notes

## 🔁 1. Loopback Address

**Range:** 127.0.0.0 – 127.255.255.255  
**Common:** 127.0.0.1 (localhost)

**Purpose:**
- Used for a device to communicate with itself
- Helps in testing network services

**Example:**
```bash
ping 127.0.0.1
```

**Key Idea:**  
Internal testing – no external network involved

---

## 🔗 2. APIPA (Automatic Private IP Addressing)

**Range:** 169.254.0.0 – 169.254.255.255  

**Purpose:**
- Assigned automatically when DHCP server is unavailable
- Enables local network communication only

**Important Note:**
- Not specifically designed for P2P
- Can allow limited peer-to-peer communication

**Key Idea:**  
Backup IP when no DHCP server is found

---

## 🌍 3. Public IP Address

**Purpose:**
- Used for communication over the internet
- Assigned by Internet Service Provider (ISP)

**Characteristics:**
- Globally unique
- Required for accessing external networks

**Key Idea:**  
Your device’s identity on the internet

---

## 🏠 4. Private IP Address

**Ranges:**
- 10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255
- 172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255
- 192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255

**Purpose:**
- Used within local area networks (LAN)

**Characteristics:**
- Not routable on the internet
- Assigned by routers (DHCP)

**Key Idea:**  
Internal network addressing

---

## ⚡ Summary Table

| Type      | Range                          | Usage                    |
|----------|--------------------------------|--------------------------|
| Loopback | 127.0.0.0 – 127.255.255.255    | Self communication       |
| APIPA    | 169.254.0.0 – 169.254.255.255  | No DHCP fallback         |
| Private  | 10.x / 172.16–31.x / 192.168.x | Local network            |
| Public   | Varies                         | Internet communication   |

---

## 🧠 Quick Memory Trick

- Loopback → Me talking to myself  
- APIPA → No DHCP, emergency IP  
- Private → Inside network  
- Public → Internet identity  
